WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.609
"And in South America tonight, an environmental problem

00:00:02.609 --> 00:00:04.200
of a much greater magnitude."

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:08.280
"The destruction of the Amazon rainforest."

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:10.100
"A worldwide disaster."

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:13.820
In the 1980s, the world learned that the Amazon was in danger.

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:15.760
"Trees are falling at a startling rate..."

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:18.220
"77,000 square miles..."

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:20.440
"... an area twice the size of Belgium..."

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:21.880
"...the size of New York State..."

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:24.420
"...the size of California, disappears."

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:27.040
And why it was so important to save it.

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:28.920
"One-fifth of the oxygen we breathe."

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:30.760
"20% of the world's fresh water."

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:33.320
"Half of the species of life on earth is in these forests."

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:37.140
"...An ecosystem the entire world needs for its survival."

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:40.300
By the 1990s, it seemed like it was too late.

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:42.100
"The destruction accelerates."

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:44.940
"More than twice as fast as previously believed."

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:47.400
"Virtually impossible to control."

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:50.280
"Once it is gone, it is gone forever."

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:52.100
Then, something changed:

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:55.800
"The annual destruction rate of the Amazon rainforest has dropped...

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:57.180
...by 70%."

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:00.500
"The lowest rate of deforestation since records began."

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:02.920
"The Amazon could achieve the end of deforestation.

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:04.000
A huge accomplishment."

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:06.700
But in order to keep it safe, there
was one condition:

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:10.080
"Protecting the forest is a continual process."

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:15.660
"Brazil will need to stay vigilant."

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:16.880
But it didn’t.

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:18.600
"The Amazon is burning."

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:20.180
"Consumed by fire."

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:21.480
"Fires have been raging."

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:22.820
"Thousands of fires are blazing..."

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:24.520
"...as more and more trees are cut down."

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:28.460
"Deforestation of the Amazon rainforest hit its highest rate in a decade."

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:32.200
Today, the Amazon is being destroyed, all over again.

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:33.560
The question is:

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:36.720
Can it be saved this time?

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:51.340
The first wave of deforestation started in
the 1970s.

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:56.870
That’s when Brazil’s military regime saw
the potential for profit deep in the Amazon.

00:01:56.870 --> 00:02:02.060
There were almost 5 million square kilometers
of rainforest filled with natural resources.

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:04.200
"Amazonia’s ores and minerals,

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:07.980
food, fiber, and forest resources are vast."

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:09.560
But most of it was inaccessible.

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:12.880
So the government started building the Trans-Amazonian Highway

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:14.510
an ambitious project that would

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:19.220
run for 3200 kilometers connecting remote
parts of the rainforest.

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:22.870
At the time, most of Brazil’s population lived in the southeast; in cities like

00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:24.780
Rio de Janeiro and São Paulo.

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:29.840
And the government wanted to move people out
here - to cultivate the land and grow the economy.

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:34.660
So they offered free land along the highway
and paid Brazilians to settle deeper in the rainforest.

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:38.820
And they sparked a land rush.

00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:46.840
As the road advanced, settlers followed, rapidly clearing the forest around it.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:53.270
Most of them turned the land into pasture,
where they raised cows to sell as beef.

00:02:53.270 --> 00:02:58.120
And when these ranchers needed more land,
they seized another plot, cleared it, and moved their cattle in.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:02.240
This expansion deeper into the Amazon drove up deforestation.

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:09.200
Between 1978 and 1988, an average of over 20,000
square kilometers were cut down each year.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:13.170
Over time this area became known as the Arc
of Deforestation.

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:17.160
And soon, a different product pushed this
even further.

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:21.099
People around the world were eating more meat, decade after decade.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:27.560
That trend raised the need for more soybeans; which served as high-protein feed for farmed animals.

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:31.920
This created a huge opportunity
for countries most suitable for growing soybeans,

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:34.180
and Brazil cashed in.

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:39.160
Soybean exports from Brazil shot up in the
mid '90s, boosting the economy.

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:45.460
By the early 2000s, farmers took over these
pastures and turned them into massive soy farms.

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:48.620
Like this one, in Acre state.

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:09.170
And the ranchers who sold their pastures,
moved their cattle further into the rainforest,

00:04:09.170 --> 00:04:11.739
clearing more of the Amazon, often illegally.

00:04:11.740 --> 00:04:16.560
This aggressive expansion created a profitable
pattern in the Amazon.

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:27.339
But it came at the cost of the rainforest.

00:04:27.339 --> 00:04:32.240
By the early 2000s, Brazil’s beef and soy
industries were driving a booming economy,

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:36.500
as well as unprecedented rates of deforestation...

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:40.520
Which caused this arc to expand further north.

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:47.340
The staggering deforestation in the Amazon
attracted fierce resistance from environmental groups.

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:50.580
"An area of ancient forest, the size of a football field

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:53.080
is destroyed every two seconds."

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:57.320
The Brazilian government, under president
Lula da Silva, finally stepped in.

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:08.620
This is Marina Silva.

00:05:08.620 --> 00:05:15.120
She was Brazil's Environment Minister in 2003,
when she helped craft a plan to stop deforestation.

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:21.900
It started with the government expanding the amount of rainforest under protection.

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:27.660
At the time, only about 28% was protected, and there was very little oversight.

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:32.100
But this new plan added more reserves, where business activities were strictly banned,

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:38.060
and also created more sustainable-use reserves,
where some businesses,

00:05:38.060 --> 00:05:44.240
like Brazillian nut harvesting and rubber-tapping, which didn’t destroy the rainforest, were allowed.

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:49.159
More land was also demarcated for indigenous
people, who preserved the forest.

00:05:49.159 --> 00:05:53.419
Over time, hundreds of new protected lands
were added, transforming the Amazon into a

00:05:53.420 --> 00:05:56.280
shared and sustainable space.

00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:14.620
Eventually, almost half the Amazon would be
put under some form of protection,

00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:18.479
while the rest of it remained a mix of pasture,
farms and rainforest.

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:22.640
To prevent further deforestation here, the government strengthened the Forest Code:

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:26.720
which said landowners could
only clear 20% of their private land.

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:31.100
This law was monitored by the Forest Service,
which was part of the Environmental Ministry,

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:33.900
which had jurisdiction over all of these protected lands.

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:37.800
And the key to enforcing this entire plan
was strengthening IBAMA:

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:42.020
a police agency that would track and fine people for illegal deforestation.

00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:00.100
And the plan showed results:

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:04.920
with deforestation rates falling by more than half in 2006.

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:11.060
At the same time, an activist movement was
forcing the agricultural industry

00:07:11.060 --> 00:07:12.780
to make a change.

00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:18.379
Major food companies started feeling pressure
from consumers for participating in deforestation.

00:07:18.379 --> 00:07:23.639
So several got together, and in 2006, signed a Soy Moratorium: which meant they could continue to operate

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:29.680
within existing farms, but they wouldn't buy
soy from any newly deforested land in the Amazon.

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:33.100
Three years later, beef companies signed a
similar agreement.

00:07:33.100 --> 00:07:37.960
Other countries also gave Brazil money to
help it protect the Amazon.

00:07:37.960 --> 00:07:42.770
Under all this protection, deforestation rates
plummeted to historic lows.

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:51.440
And yet, Brazil’s soy and beef industries
continued to grow, thanks to more efficient techniques:

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:54.320
Ranchers started growing crops on their existing
pastures.

00:07:54.540 --> 00:07:58.800
And farmers planted two crops a year on their
land instead of one.

00:07:58.809 --> 00:08:03.599
Brazil had found a way make to Amazon both
productive, and protected.

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:07.180
But there were some who still wanted it to
be a more profitable place.

00:08:10.200 --> 00:08:15.880
The ruralistas, a group of conservative politicians who represent the interests of the agricultural industry,

00:08:15.880 --> 00:08:20.099
including farmers and ranchers, started gaining influence in Brazil.

00:08:20.099 --> 00:08:24.319
In the early 2000s they had about 17% of the
seats in congress.

00:08:24.319 --> 00:08:27.409
But by 2012 they had about 30%...

00:08:27.409 --> 00:08:31.380
Enough power to push President Dilma Rousseff
to weaken the Forest Code,

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:36.160
which allowed landowners to get away with clearing more land.

00:08:36.160 --> 00:08:41.520
In 2016, they pushed President Michel Temer
to slash IBAMA's budget.

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:45.180
They also helped him pass a law that made
it easier for people who illegally seized

00:08:45.190 --> 00:08:48.160
land in the Amazon, to keep it.

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:52.920
These changes emboldened some people to seize and clear the rainforest again.

00:08:53.080 --> 00:08:56.340
And that led to a rise in deforestation rates.

00:08:56.340 --> 00:09:00.940
In 2018, as the ruralistas controlled 44%
of Congress,

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:06.580
Jair Bolsonaro, a right-wing congressman and ally to the ruralistas, was elected president.

00:09:15.620 --> 00:09:19.060
On his second day in office, he transferred
the forest service, which monitors the

00:09:19.060 --> 00:09:23.900
forest code, to the agricultural ministry
- led by a ruralista.

00:09:23.900 --> 00:09:27.500
He's also worked to systematically weaken
the Environmental Ministry.

00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:34.520
Under Bolsonaro, deforestation has increased
significantly in 2019,

00:09:34.530 --> 00:09:38.800
most of it taking place in these protected
areas.

00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:41.340
Setting fires is a common way to clear land...

00:09:41.340 --> 00:09:46.100
And in August 2019, over 30,000 fires were
burning in the Amazon:

00:09:46.100 --> 00:09:48.820
Three times as many as in August 2018.

00:09:48.820 --> 00:09:54.800
Many set illegally by ranchers, farmers, and
landowners, emboldened by the government's new stance on the Amazon.

00:09:54.820 --> 00:09:59.920
But this time, the Amazon is unlikely to survive
another wave of deforestation.

00:10:05.580 --> 00:10:11.220
In the last 50 years, it's estimated that about 17% of the Amazon has been deforested.

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:17.840
A 2018 report estimated that, if it reaches
20-25%, the whole rainforest could start to collapse.

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:22.020
It wouldn't be enough to cycle all the
water it needs, causing trees to die.

00:10:22.020 --> 00:10:27.440
And that would release a huge amount of carbon
dioxide into the atmosphere, further warming the planet.

00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:32.320
But in Brazil, many politicians and agricultural
businesses continue to ignore the science

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:34.340
for the sake of profit.

00:11:01.880 --> 00:11:04.720
Clearing the Amazon for short-term gains overlooks the fact

00:11:04.720 --> 00:11:09.420
that the planet as we know it wouldn't exist without this rainforest.

00:11:09.420 --> 00:11:12.880
It’s why this place was saved 
once before.

00:11:17.620 --> 00:11:19.620
Thanks for watching this special edition of Vox Atlas.

00:11:19.620 --> 00:11:22.140
This is one of three that we produced on the Amazon.

00:11:22.140 --> 00:11:25.260
And this one was about the drivers of deforestation and

00:11:25.260 --> 00:11:28.140
and some of the Brazilian politics surrounding the current crisis today.

00:11:28.140 --> 00:11:31.100
In the next one we're going to take a step back in the history

00:11:31.100 --> 00:11:33.100
We're going to meet a man named Chico Mendes

00:11:33.100 --> 00:11:36.820
who led the original fight to save the Amazon, back in the 1980s.

00:11:36.820 --> 00:11:40.420
His story is super interesting and very relevant to today.

00:11:40.420 --> 00:11:42.920
So make sure to come back and watch more Vox Atlas in the Amazon.

